# modules.cpp touch input interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.1 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented portable touch input interface and its CST328 controller driver.
mm.touch, the CST328 driver, the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board provider, and the
board-smoke application exist. Touch behavior on hardware remains a
hardware-qualification requirement rather than a completed claim.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented mm.mcu
interface, including the I2C and GPIO transports this driver uses.
docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains authoritative for SDK and board selection.
docs/modules-mm.mdy remains authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

# Decision

Touch input is a device service layered above mm.mcu I2C and GPIO. It is not
a pointer device, an event queue, a gesture engine, and not a policy module.

The intended dependency direction is:

Opening fence
portable application
        |
     mm.touch
        |
selected board touch provider
        |
mm.touch.cst328 (or another controller)
        |
I2C and GPIO from mm.mcu
        |
selected MCU platform provider
Closing fence

An application using only portable touch behavior declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.touch
Closing fence

It does not name a controller, vendor, I2C address, GPIO number, or provider
module. The selected physical board profile supplies those facts.

# Portable interface

mm.touch is a platform interface with an allocation-free API for geometry,
initialization, point readings, and sleep. Its fallback object reports
Unsupported.

The public shape is:

Opening fence
enum class Status { Ok, BadArgument, Unsupported,
                    NotInitialized, Busy, Timeout, TransportError };

struct Geometry {
    unsigned int width = 0;
    unsigned int height = 0;
    unsigned int points = 0;
};

struct Point {
    unsigned int x = 0;
    unsigned int y = 0;
};

class Touch {
public:
    virtual ~Touch() = default;

    [[nodiscard]] virtual Geometry geometry() const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status read(std::span<Point>, std::size_t& count);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status sleep();
};

void set_touch(Touch&);
[[nodiscard]] Touch& selected_touch();
Closing fence

`read` fills as many points as the panel currently reports, up to the caller's
span, and sets count. No contact is Ok with count zero rather than an error:
nothing being touched is an answer. A caller never learns about points it had
no room for, so a span of one is a valid single-touch client of a five-point
panel. `count` is written only when the call answers Ok, so a caller that
ignores the status cannot mistake a stale count for a fresh reading.

`Geometry` describes the panel's own coordinate space: width, height, and the
maximum number of simultaneous contacts the panel reports. A panel laminated
to a display need not share its geometry; rotation and calibration belong to
the caller, because only the caller knows how the panel is mounted relative
to what is drawn on it.

The interrupt line a controller may offer is deliberately absent. A caller
polls `read`; a provider may use its own interrupt internally to make that
cheap. Exposing an interrupt would mean exposing a callback registration
mm.mcu does not have.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board touch provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.touch.

# Controller driver

mm.touch.cst328 implements the Hynitron CST328 capacitive touch controller
over portable mm.mcu I2C and GPIO. It contains no vendor header and no board
pin number: a board's provider supplies the wiring and this module drives the
protocol.

The driver receives immutable wiring and panel descriptors:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::I2cConfiguration i2c;
    unsigned int address;
    unsigned int reset_gpio;
    unsigned int interrupt_gpio;
    unsigned long reset_hold_ms;
    unsigned long reset_release_ms;
};

struct Panel {
    unsigned int width;
    unsigned int height;
    unsigned int points;  // default 5
};
Closing fence

The register map is sixteen-bit and big-endian, which is why every access is
an mm.mcu `i2c_write_read` rather than a write followed by a read: the
controller's address pointer must not be exposed to another master between the
two halves.

Reset holds the reset GPIO low for `reset_hold_ms`, releases it, and waits
`reset_release_ms`. Both are panel timings rather than bus timings, so a board
that measured different ones may say so.

The driver owns a state machine distinguishing Idle, Ready, and Sleeping.
Initialization configures I2C, performs the reset sequence, identifies the
controller, and reaches Ready. `read` queries the contact registers and
decomposes the packed big-endian coordinates into Points. Sleep puts the
controller in its low-power state; a subsequent read requires reinitialization.

Two things in the Waveshare reference driver for the RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8 are
deliberately not reproduced. Its interrupt handler is absent, because
mm.touch is polled and mm.mcu has no callback registration. Its rotation
transform is absent, because mm.touch reports the panel's own coordinates and
rotation belongs to whoever knows how the panel is mounted.

# Provider binding

mm.touch carries the existing platform-interface marker. A concrete hardware
board binds it to one unique provider:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.touch platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.touch
Closing fence

The provider imports mm.touch, the selected controller module, and mm.mcu. It
owns the fixed I2C wiring, reset and interrupt GPIOs, and panel geometry,
constructs the controller, and registers the Touch implementation from a
retained static object. Existing provider validation, board-over-SDK
precedence, conditional closure injection, and core platform-edge rules apply
unchanged.

# Test strategy

Controller tests use a fake MCU Platform that records I2C and GPIO operations.
Tests compare semantic register transcripts rather than merely checking that
a source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- reset sequence and GPIO timing;
- initialization transcript for the exact controller revision;
- contact register reading and big-endian coordinate decomposition;
- zero-contact reading answers Ok with count zero;
- partial span: a span of two from a five-point panel fills two points;
- sleep and the reinitialization path;
- transport errors propagated without continuing the register sequence;
- no touch or provider objects in an unrelated executable closure.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms exactly one touch and
MCU provider registration object. It then flashes through flash.sh.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.mcu:i2c with host transcript tests.
2. Implemented: add mm.touch with a host stand-in independent of any
   controller.
3. Implemented: add mm.touch.cst328 with reset, read, and sleep tests.
4. Implemented: add the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board touch provider.
5. Implemented: add apps/board-smoke using mm.touch alongside mm.display,
   mm.imu, and mm.rtc.
6. Pending hardware: qualify contact detection, multi-point reading, and
   sleep on the named Waveshare board.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.touch links with exactly one selected touch
  provider and the provider's controller closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no touch binding.
- An app not importing mm.touch contains no touch provider or controller
  registration object.
- Controller host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- I2C register accesses cross the platform boundary as write-read
  transactions, not separate write and read calls.
- No controller source includes vendor or Pico SDK headers.
- A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful touch reading.

# What this does not settle

- CST328 is the first implemented controller. Its interrupt-driven mode,
  pen/stylus support, and palm rejection are absent.
- Gesture recognition, event queues, pointer state, and rotation are policy
  above this module.
- Multi-touch beyond the panel's reported point count is not promised.
- A data-ready interrupt is not exposed; mm.mcu has no callback registration.
- Multi-panel selection and runtime touch discovery are not specified.
